\details{
\itemize{
\item \code{ancombc_global()}: does the feature differ between any of the
levels? A Wald-type test of all the variable's coefficients at once.
\item \code{ancombc_pairwise()}: every level against the reference, and every
pair of the other levels, with the mixed directional false discovery
rate (mdFDR) screened by the global test.
\item \code{ancombc_dunnett()}: every level against the reference only, with
mdFDR screened by a bootstrapped global test (Dunnett's type).
\item \code{ancombc_trend()}: does the feature follow an ordered pattern across
the levels (by default increasing or decreasing)? Constrained
estimates, with a bootstrapped p-value.
}

With \eqn{\hat\beta_f} the feature's \eqn{k} coefficients of \code{groups}
and \eqn{V_f} their covariance matrix (from the fit):
\itemize{
\item \strong{Global test:} \eqn{W = \hat\beta' V^+ \hat\beta}, referred to
\eqn{\chi^2_k} (\code{ancombc()}) or \eqn{F_{k, \nu}} (\code{ancombc2()}, with
the feature's residual degrees of freedom), with the two-sided p-value
\eqn{2 \min(P, 1 - P)}, as in ANCOMBC and scikit-bio.
\item \strong{Pairwise and Dunnett's tests:} each comparison's \eqn{W =
  \hat\delta / \mathrm{se}}, against the normal or t distribution as in
the fit. mdFDR (Guo, Sarkar & Peddada 2010): a global screen at
\code{alpha}, with Benjamini-Hochberg across features (the global test for
pairwise; for Dunnett's, the largest \eqn{|W|} against \code{n_bootstraps}
draws of the largest of \eqn{k} null t or normal values); then each
screened feature's comparisons are adjusted with \code{p_adjust} for a
family of \eqn{m D / R} tests (\eqn{m} comparisons, \eqn{D} features,
\eqn{R} passing the screen).
\item \strong{Trend test:} for each pattern, the estimate closest to
\eqn{\hat\beta} in the metric \eqn{V^{-1}} that satisfies its
constraints (a small quadratic programme); its statistic is
\eqn{\max(|\beta_{node}|, |\beta_{node} - \beta_k|)}, and \eqn{W} the
largest over the patterns. The p-value is the proportion of
\code{n_bootstraps} null draws (standard normal coefficients, projected the
same way and scaled by the standard errors) whose statistic exceeds
\eqn{W}; then \code{p_adjust} across features.
}

Features whose coefficients can't be estimated get \code{NaN} statistics and a
p-value of 1.

\strong{Random numbers.} The bootstraps use ecodive's own generator (PCG),
with \code{seed}: one stream per feature, so results are identical for any
\code{cpus} and a feature's results don't depend on the others.
\code{ancombc_global()} and \code{ancombc_pairwise()} draw nothing.
}
\section{Differences from scikit-bio and ANCOMBC}{

The statistics and deterministic p-values are scikit-bio's (0.7.4), to
about \code{1e-10} given the same fit; the bootstrapped p-values agree with
scikit-bio's in distribution (they agree exactly given the same null
draws), not draw for draw, since the random numbers differ. The trend
test's constrained estimates are the exact solutions of its quadratic
programmes, which scikit-bio's SLSQP and ANCOMBC's \code{quadprog}
approximate. Comparisons are labelled with level names (scikit-bio:
\verb{status[T.severe]_status[T.moderate]}), and \code{groups} can differ from
the fit's.
}

